{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1523412231,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE1Njc1MzAyMDE="},"added":["question"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1523423800,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM4MDMyOTkwOQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2411#issuecomment-380329909"},"message":"ad 1: discontinue split between metadata and metadata-extra -- not sure (TM). I think about it this way: what do I need to install if I want to work with provided metadata? What do I need to install if I want to extract metadata myself? I would be all for renaming -extra to `metadata-extraction` or something like this, to make this clearer. That being said, I don't know how much of a difference that makes in real life...\n\nad 2: warning v failure. It used to be warning, and I changed this in response to a comment by @yarikoptic He was arguing along the lines of \"a warning will get lost among the many messages, and the result will be an unintentionally crippled, unnoticed metadata record\". Never listen to the whiners...\n \n5 hours is still annoying. Two approaches: 1) avoid monolithic aggregation of hierarchies of 500+ datasets ;-) 2) it should be relatively easy to build the list of required extractors upfront, and fail right away if some are not available. This will come at the cost of being less \"responsive\", generator-like. However, failing in the middle of things (status quo) isn't a convenient result either...","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1523447676,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM4MDQyNTQ3MQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2411#issuecomment-380425471"},"message":"Ha ha, I did feel that he indeed complained the other way around, but I no longer trust his whining ;-)","files":null},{"type":3,"a